\begin_layout Section*
7.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\shape up
Brouwer's Fixed Point Theorem.

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 be a non-empty, compact, and convex subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

.
 If a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f:X\rightarrow X$
\end_inset

 is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 has a fixed point, i.e., there is a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x^{*})=x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Kakutani's Fixed Point Theorem.

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 be a non-empty, compact, and convex subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{m}$
\end_inset

.
 If a correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F:X\rightarrow2^{X}$
\end_inset

 is an upper hemi-continuous correspondence with non-empty compact and convex
 values on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 has a fixed point, i.e., there is a point 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in X$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in F(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
FKKM Theorem.

\series default
 
\emph on
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be a convex set and 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset\nsubseteq X\subseteq Y$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $F:X\rightarrow2^{Y}$
\end_inset

 is a correspondence such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $F(x)$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
is closed for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $F(x_{0})$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
is compact for some 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{0}\in X$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
is FS-convex, i.e., for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{m}\in X$
\end_inset

 and its convex combination 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}=\sum_{i=1}^{m}\lambda_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\in\cup_{i=1}^{m}F(x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\cap_{x\in X}F(x)\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
7.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Structure of the General Equilibrium Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A 
\emph on
general equilibrium
\emph default
 is defined as a state where the aggregate demand does not excess the aggregate
 supply for all markets.
 Thus, equilibrium prices are endogenously determined.
 The general equilibrium approach has to central features:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It views the economy as a closed and inter-related system in which we must
 simultaneously determine the equilibrium values of all variables of interest
 (i.e., consider all markets together).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It aims at reducing the set of variables taken as exogenous to a small number
 of physical realities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use the general equilibrium approach to predict the final consumption
 and production in the market mechansim.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Economic Environments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The fundamentals of the economy are economic institutional environments
 that are exogenously given and characterized by the following terms:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $n:$
\end_inset

 the number of consumers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $N=\{1,\ldots,n\}:$
\end_inset

 the set of consumers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $J:$
\end_inset

 the number of producers (firms)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $L:$
\end_inset

 the number of (private) goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}:$
\end_inset

 the consumption space of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, which specifies the boundary of consumptions, collection of all individually
 feasible consumption of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Some components of an element may be negative such as labor supply
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}:$
\end_inset

 Preference ordering (or 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 if a utility function exists) of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Remark:
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a preference ordering if it is reflexive (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

), transitive (
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none

\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset


\family default
\series default
\shape default
\size default
\emph default
\bar default
\strikeout default
\uuline default
\uwave default
\noun default
\color inherit
 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{i}^{\prime\prime}$
\end_inset

), and complete (for any pair 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, either 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x_{i}$
\end_inset

 or both).
 It can be shown that it can be represented by a continuous utility funciton
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
 The existence of general equilibrium can be obtained even when preferences
 are weakened to be non-complete or non-transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\in X_{i}:$
\end_inset

 initial endowment vector of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=(X_{i},\succsim_{i},w_{i}):$
\end_inset

 the characteristics of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}:$
\end_inset

 production possibility set of firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,2,\ldots,J$
\end_inset

, which is the characteristic of producer 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\in Y_{j}:$
\end_inset

 a production plan, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{jl}>0$
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{jl}$
\end_inset

 is output and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{jl}<0$
\end_inset

 means 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{jl}$
\end_inset

 is input.
 Most elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}$
\end_inset

 for a firm are zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Remark:
\series default
 
\emph default
Recall that there can be three types of returns about production scales:
 non-increasing returns to scale (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

), non-decreasing returns to scale (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq1$
\end_inset

), and constant returns to scale (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\geq0$
\end_inset

).
 In other words, non-increasing returns to scale implies any feasible input-outp
ut vector can be scaled down; non-decreasing returns to scale implies any
 feasible input-output vector can be scaled up, and constant returns to
 scale implies the production set is the conjuntion of non-increasing and
 non-decreasing returns.
 With non-decreasing returns to scale, firms' profits are infinite, so we
 will avoid this case in the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e=\left(\left\{ X_{i},\succsim_{i},w_{i}\right\} ,\left\{ Y_{j}\right\} \right):$
\end_inset

 an economy, or also called an economic environment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $X=X_{1}\times\cdots\times X_{n}:$
\end_inset

 consumption space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Y=Y_{1}\times\cdots\times Y_{J}:$
\end_inset

 production space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Institutional Arrangement: Private Market Mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p=(p_{1},\ldots,p_{L})^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:$
\end_inset

 a price vector
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x_{i}:$
\end_inset

 the expenditure of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j}:$
\end_inset

 the profit of firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot w_{i}:$
\end_inset

 the value of endowments of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:$
\end_inset

 the profit share of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

, which specifies ownership (property rights) structures, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}\theta_{ij}=1$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i's$
\end_inset

 budget constraint is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot x_{i}\leq p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the budget set is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
B_{i}(p)=\left\{ x_{i}\in X_{i}:p\cdot x_{i}\leq p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A private ownership economy then is referred to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
e=\left(e_{1},\ldots,e_{n},\left\{ Y_{j}\right\} _{j=1}^{J},\left\{ \theta_{ij}\right\} \right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The set of all such private ownership economies are denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $E$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.2.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Individual Behavior Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Perfect competitive markets: Every player is a price-taker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Utility maximization: Every consumer maximizes his preferences subject to
 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{i}}}\ u_{i}(x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot x_{i}\leq p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Profit maximization: Every firm maximizes its profit in 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{y_{j}\in Y_{j}}}\ p\cdot y_{j}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.2.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Competitive Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Before defining the notion of competitive equilibrium, we first give some
 information on allocations, which identify the set of possible outcomes
 in economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

.
 For notational convenience, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}$
\end_inset

 will be used throughout the notes to denote the sum of vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{a}=\sum a_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An 
\series bold
\emph on
allocation
\series default
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is a specification of a consumption vector 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n})^{\prime}\in X$
\end_inset

 and production vector 
\begin_inset Formula $y=(y_{1},\ldots y_{J})^{\prime}\in Y$
\end_inset

.
 An allocation is 
\series bold
\emph on
individually feasible
\series default
\emph default
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\in X_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in N$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J$
\end_inset

.
 An allocation is 
\series bold
\emph on
weakly balanced
\series default
\emph default
 if
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{x}\leq\hat{y}+\hat{w}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or specifically
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{j}}y_{j}+{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

When inequality holds with equality, the allocation is called a 
\series bold
\emph on
balanced
\series default
\emph default
 or 
\series bold
\emph on
attainable
\series default
\emph default
 allocation, i.e., aggregate demand equals aggregate supply.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is 
\series bold
\emph on
feasible
\series default
\emph default
 if it is both individually feasible and (weakly) balanced.
 Thus, an economic allocation is feasible if the total amount of each good
 consumed does not exceed the total amount available from both the initial
 endowment and production.
 Denote the set of all feasible allocations by 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\left\{ (x,y)\in X\times Y:\hat{x}\leq\hat{y}+\hat{w}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now define the notion of competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
7.2.1 (Competitive Equilibrium or also called Walrasian Equilibrium).
 
\series default
Given a private ownership economy, 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(e_{1},\ldots,e_{n},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

, an allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)\in X\times Y$
\end_inset

 and a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 constitutes a competitive equilibrium if the following conditions are satisfied
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Utility maximization: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N,\exists\ x_{i}\in B_{i}(p),\forall\ x_{i}^{\prime}\in B_{i}(p):x_{i}\succsim x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Profit maximization: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ j,\ \exists\ y_{j}\in Y_{j},\forall\ y_{j}^{\prime}\in Y_{j}:p\cdot y_{j}\geq p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Market clearing condition: 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}\leq\hat{w}+\hat{y}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Note*
To find a CE in a production economy, follow the following steps: (1) profit
 maximization; (2) utility maximization; (3) market clearing condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Denote
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)=\left\{ x_{i}\in B_{i}(p):\forall\ x_{i}^{\prime}\in B_{i}(p),\ x_{i}\succsim_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right\} :$
\end_inset

 the demand correspondence of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 under utility maximization; it is called the demand function of consumer
 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 if it is a single-valued funciton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)=\left\{ y_{j}\in Y_{j}:\forall\ y_{j}^{\prime}\in Y_{j},\ p\cdot y_{j}\geq p\cdot y_{j}^{\prime}\right\} :$
\end_inset

 the supply correspondence of the firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

; it is called the supply function of firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 if it is a single-valued function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, we can define the following
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}(p)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}(p):$
\end_inset

 the aggregate demand correpondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}(p)=\sum_{j=1}^{J}y_{j}(p):$
\end_inset

 the aggregate supply correspondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}-\hat{y}(p):$
\end_inset

 the aggregate excess demand correspondence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we have correspondences, we just add the sets together, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $X+Y=\left\{ x+y:x\in X\ \wedge\ y\in Y\right\} $
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An equivalent definition of a competitive equilibrium is that a price vector
 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector if there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}\in\hat{z}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is single-valued, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

 is a comeptitive equilibrium.
 Note, competitive equilibria can still exist if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a correspondence instead of a function.
 If we do not assume the production possibility sets satisfy free disposal,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}=\hat{y}+\hat{w}\iff\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

 and the budget constraint holds with equality for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
7.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Examples of GE Models: Graphical Treatment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In most economies, there are three types of economic activities: produciton,
 consumption, and exchanges.
 Before formally stating the existence results on competitive equilibrium,
 we will first give two simple examples of general equilibrium models: exchange
 economies and a production economy with only one consumer and one firm.
 These examples introduce some of the questions, concepts, and common techniques
 that will occupy us for the rest of this part.
 Remember, absolute prices do not matter, only relative prices matter, i.e.,
 the ratio of prices 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{p_{1}}{p_{2}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Pure Exchange Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A pure exchange economy is an economy in which there is no production.
 This is a special case of a general economy.
 In this case, economic activies only consist of trading and consumption.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The aggregate excess demand correspondence becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=\hat{x}(p)-\hat{w}$
\end_inset

 so that we can define the individual excess demand by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}(p)=x_{i}(p)-w_{i}$
\end_inset

 for this special case.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The simplest exchange economy with the possibility of mutual benefit exchange
 is the two-good and two-consumer exchange economy.
 Note that the point denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in the Edgeworth Box can be used to represent the initial endowments of
 two people.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Edgeworth Box
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider an excahnge economy with two goods 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 and two persons.
 The total endowment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}=w_{1}+w_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 For example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(1,2)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(3,1)$
\end_inset

, then the total endowment is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}=(4,3)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that the point, denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 in the Edgeworth Box, can be used to represent the initial endowments of
 two persons.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The advantage of the Edgeworth Box is that it gives all the possible (balanced)
 trading points.
 That is,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}+x_{2}=w_{1}+w_{2}
\]

\end_inset

for all points 
\begin_inset Formula $x=(x_{1},x_{2})$
\end_inset

 in the box, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=(x_{11},x_{12})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=(x_{21},x_{22})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, every point in the Edgeworth Box stands for an attainable allocation
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}+x_{2}=w_{1}+w_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The shaded lens (portion) of the box represents all the trading points that
 make both persons better off.
 Beyond the box, any point is not feasible.
 In the box, one person's budget line is also the budget line of the other
 person.
 They share the same budget line in the box.
 In the interior equilibrium case, two indifference curves are tangent to
 each other at a point that is on the budget line so that the marginal rates
 of substitutions for the two persons are the same and equal to the price
 ratio, and aggregate demand equals aggregate supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A competitive equilibrium may still exist even if two persons' indifference
 curves are not tangent.
 In this case, there is no tangent point as long as the slopes of the indifferen
ce curves of the two persons are not the same.
 Even so, there sill exists a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series bold
\emph on
Offer curve:
\series default
\emph default
 The locus of the optimal consumptions for the two goods when price varies.
 Note that it consists of tangent points of the indifference curves and
 budget lines when price varies.
 The intersection of the two offer curves of the consumers can be used to
 check if there is a competitive equilibrium.
 One intersection of two consumers' offer curves is always given at the
 endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 If there is another intersection of two consumers' offer curves rather
 than the one at the endowment 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

, the intersection point must be the competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that a preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is convex if 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $tx_{i}+(1-t)x_{i}^{\prime}\succ x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i},x_{i}^{\prime}\in X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 has a satiation point 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\succsim x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}\in X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Cases in which there may be no Walrasian Equiliria
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
Indifference curves are not convex.
 If the two offer curves are not intersected except for at the endowment
 points, then there may not exist a competitive equilibrium.
 This may be true when preferences are not convex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
The initial endowment may not be an interior point of the consumption space.
 Consider an exchange economy in which one person's indifference curves
 put no values on one commodity, like when agent 2's indifference curves
 are vertical lines so that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{2}(x_{21},x_{22})=x_{21}$
\end_inset

.
 Agent 1's utility function is a regular one, which is given by a quasi-linear
 utility function, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}=(x_{11},x_{12})=\sqrt{x_{11}}+x_{12}$
\end_inset

.
 The initial endowments are given by 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(1,0)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 There is no competitive equilibrium.
 Why? There are two cases to be considered.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}=0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Agent 1 would demand infinite amount of the good whose price is zero.
 Thus, there is no competitive equilibrium.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Agent 2's demand for good 1 is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{21}=1$
\end_inset

, and agent 1 would demand a positive quantity of good 1 because of the
 quasi-linear utility function.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{MU_{1}}{p_{1}}=\frac{MU_{2}}{p_{2}}\iff\frac{1}{2p_{1}\sqrt{x_{1}}}=\frac{1}{p_{2}}
\]

\end_inset

 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}\approx0$
\end_inset

, then the left-hand side approaches infinity, and the left-hand side is
 greater than the right-hand side.
 So, to obtain equality, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}>0$
\end_inset

.
 As such, the aggregate demand for good 1 is greater than 1, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}(p)+x_{21}(p)=\hat{x}_{1}(p)>\hat{w}_{1}$
\end_inset

, which violates the feasibility conditions.
 Thus, no competitive equilibrium exists.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
7.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The One-Consumer and One-Produce Economy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we introduce the possibility of production.
 We analyze the simplest possible setting in which there are only two price-taki
ng economic agents.
 The two agents are one producer so that 
\begin_inset Formula $J=1$
\end_inset

 and one consumer so that 
\begin_inset Formula $n=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=1$
\end_inset

.
 There are also two goods: labor (leisure) and the consumption good produced
 by the firm.
 
\begin_inset Formula $w=(\bar{L},0)$
\end_inset

 is the consumer's endowment, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{L}$
\end_inset

 denotes the total units of leisure time.
 We also have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $f(z):$
\end_inset

 the production funciton is strictly increasing, concave, and differentiable,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is labor input.
 To have an interior solution, we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Inada conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{z\rightarrow0}f^{\prime}(z)=+\infty$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{z\rightarrow+\infty}f^{\prime}(z)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
We need the production function to be concave, not quasi-concave.
 For example, if 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)=z^{2},\ z\geq0$
\end_inset

, then we have a quasi-concave function, but it is also convex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\omega):$
\end_inset

 the price vector of the consumption good and labor.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u(x_{1},x_{2}):$
\end_inset

 is the utility function which is quasi-concave, increasing, and differentiable.
 To have an interior solution, we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Inada conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{x_{i}\rightarrow0}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{i}}(x_{i})=+\infty$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lim_{x_{i}\rightarrow+\infty}\frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{i}}(x_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $q(p,\omega):$
\end_inset

 the profit maximizing output for the consumption good
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $z(p,\omega):$
\end_inset

 the profit maximizing input for the labor
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi(p,\omega):$
\end_inset

 the profit maximizing funciton
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The firm's problem is to choose the labor 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 so as to solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{z\geq0}}\ pf(z)-\omega z
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The first-order condition is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
pf^{\prime}(z)=\omega\nonumber \\
\implies f^{\prime}(z)=\frac{\omega}{p}
\end{gather}

\end_inset

which means the marginal rate of technical substitution of labor for the
 consumption good equals the price ratio of the labor input and the consumption
 good output (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $MRTS_{z,q}=\nicefrac{\omega}{p}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The consumer's problem is to choose the leisure time and the consumption
 for the good so as to solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{1},x_{2}}}\ u(x_{1},x_{2})
\]

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
px_{2}\leq\omega(\bar{L}-x_{1})+\pi(p,\omega)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 is leisure and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 is the consumption good.
 The first-order condition is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{1}}}{\frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{2}}}=\frac{\omega}{p}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which means the marginal rate of substitution of leisure for the consumption
 good 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 equals the price ratio of the leisure and consumption good (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}=\nicefrac{\omega}{p}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By (10) and (11)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
MRS_{x_{1},x_{2}}=\frac{\omega}{p}=MRTS_{z,q}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A competitive equilibrium for this economy involves a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*},\omega^{*})$
\end_inset

 at which
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{2}(p^{*},\omega^{*}) & = & q(p^{*},\omega^{*})\\
x_{1}(p^{*},\omega^{*})+z(p^{*},\omega^{*}) & = & \bar{L}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the aggregate demand for the two goods equals the aggregate supply
 for the two goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
7.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Existence of Competitive Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The proof of the existence of a competitive equilibrium is generally considered
 one of the most important and robust results of economic theory.
 In this section, we will examine the existence of competitive equilibrium
 for the three cases: (1) the single-valued aggregate excess demand function;
 (2) the aggregate excess demand correspondence; (3) a general class of
 private ownership production economies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this section we will examine the existence of competitive equilibrium
 for the three cases: (1) the single-valued aggregate excess demand function;
 (2) the aggregate excess demand correspondence; (3) a general class of
 private ownership production economies.
 The first two cases are based on excess deman instead of underlying preference
 orderings and consumption and produciton sets.
 There are many ways to prove the existence of general equilibrium.
 For instance, one can use the Brouwer fixed point theorem approach, KKM
 lemma approach, and abstract economy approach to show the existence of
 competitive equilibrium for these three cases.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Existence of CE for Aggregate Excess Demand Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The simplest case for the existence of a competitive equilibrium is the
 one when the aggregate excess demand correspondence is a single-valued
 function.
 Note that, when preference orderings and production sets are both strictly
 convex, we obtain excess demand functions rather than correspondences.
 Also, if preferences are strictly monotone, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is not well-defined when 
\begin_inset Formula $p=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A very important property of the aggregate excess demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is Walras' law, which can take one of the following three forms:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The strong form of Walras' law is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The weak form of Walras' law is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:p\cdot\hat{z}(p)\leq0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The interior form of Walras' Law is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}:p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another important property of the aggregate excess demand funciton is homogeneit
y of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 It is homogeneous of degree zero in price, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda>0:\hat{z}(\lambda p)=\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a preference ordering, locally non-satiated, and continuous, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 Also, if 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is non-empty, closed, and satisfies free disposal, then 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
 Normally, we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is always homogenous of degree zero.
 From this property, we can normalize prices.
 For example, we can normalize a price vector as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}=\nicefrac{p_{l}}{p_{1}},\ l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 Here, good 1 is known as the numeraire, i.e., its price has been normalized
 to 1.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{\prime}=\nicefrac{p_{l}}{\sum_{l=1}^{L}}p_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 This normalization is often used in proofs of existence of CE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, without loss of generality, we can restrict our attention to the unit
 simplex:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S^{L-1}=\left\{ p^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}}p_{l}^{\prime}=1\right\} \subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}
\]

\end_inset

which is a non-empty and compact set.
 Note, the second normalization can be used in the construction of this
 set 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{\prime}\in S^{L-1})$
\end_inset

, but not the first.
 Remember, that the unit simplex is always 1 degree less than 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 So, in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{2}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 is a line, and in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}^{3}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 is a plane.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.1 (Existence Theorem I).

\series default
 
\emph on
For a private ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a homogeneous of degree zero and continuous function, and satisfies
 the strong Walras' Law, then there exists a competitive equilibrium, i.e.,
 there is 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\leq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
For this Theorem, preferences can't be strictly monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Define a continuous function 
\begin_inset Formula $g:S^{L-1}\rightarrow S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{l}=g_{l}(p)=\frac{p_{l}+\max\{0,\hat{z}_{l}(p)\}}{\sum_{k=1}^{L}\left[p_{k}+\max\{0,\hat{z}_{k}(p)\}\right]}=\frac{p_{l}+\max\{0,\hat{z}_{l}(p)\}}{1+\sum_{k=1}^{L}\max\{0,\hat{z}_{k}(p)\}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 First, we have constructed a mechanism to increase the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 when there is a shortage of the respective good (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p)>0$
\end_inset

).
 The numerator performs this task, while the denominator normalizes the
 price.
 Second, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 is a continuous function since 
\begin_inset Formula $\max\{f(x),h(x)\}$
\end_inset

 is a continuous function when 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $h(x)$
\end_inset

 are continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By Brouwer's Fixed Point Theorem, there exists a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $g(p^{*})=p^{*}$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{l}^{*}=\frac{p_{l}^{*}+\max\{0,\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\}}{1+\sum_{k=1}^{L}\max\{0,\hat{z}_{k}(p^{*})\}},\ l=1,\ldots,L
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

is in fact a competitive equilibrium price vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Cross multiplying 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\sum_{k=1}^{L}\max\{0,\hat{z}_{k}(p^{*})\}$
\end_inset

 on both sides of (14), we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{l}^{*}\sum_{k=1}^{L}\max\{0,\hat{z}_{k}(p^{*})\}=\max\{0,\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, multiplying equation (15) by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 and taking summation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}}p_{l}^{*}\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\right]\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}}\max\left\{ 0,\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\right\} \right]={\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}}\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\max\left\{ 0,\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, by the strong Walras' Law, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{L}}\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\max\left\{ 0,\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\right\} =0
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Therefore, each term of the summation is either zero or 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*}))^{2}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, to have the summation be equal to zero, we must have each term be
 zero.
 That is, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p):S^{L-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is a vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 goods, and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a scalar.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 7.4.1.

\series default
 Do not confuse competitive equilibrium of an aggregate excess demand function
 with the strong Walras' Law.
 Even though Walras' Law holds, we may not have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\leq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 Also, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a comeptitive equilibrium price vector, the strong Walras' Law may not
 hold unless some types of monotonicty are imposed, such as local non-satiation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Strong Walras' Law does not imply a competitive equilibrium, and a competitive
 equilibrium does not imply Strong Walras' Law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Fact

\series bold
\shape up
(Free goods).
 
\series default
\emph on
Under the strong Walras' Law, if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})<0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose not.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}\cdot\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})<0$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})<0$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts strong Walras' Law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Note.
 (Desirable goods).

\series default
 
\emph on
If 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Fact

\series bold
\shape up
(Equality of demand and supply).

\series default
\emph on
 If all goods are desirable and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose not.
 Then, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})<0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
 By the free goods condition, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

.
 However, since all goods are desirable, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})>0,$
\end_inset

 which contradicts 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})<0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 7.4.2.
 
\series default
By the strong Walras' Law, if 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $L-1$
\end_inset

 markets are in equilibrium, then the 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

-th market is also in the equilibrium.
 Thus, because of the strong Walras' Law, to verify that a price vector
 
\begin_inset Formula $p>0$
\end_inset

 clears all markets, it suffices to check that it clears all markets but
 one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
There are many different people in the economy with varying preferences,
 so we want to make the existence theorems of CE's as general as possible
 by weakening the conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.2 (Existence Theorem I').

\series default
 
\emph on
For a private ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

, if the aggregate excess demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a lower semi-continuous function and satisfies strong or weak Walras'
 Law, then there exists a competitive equilibrium, i.e., there is 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S\equiv S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Define a correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F:S\rightarrow2^{S}$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ p\in S:F(p)=\left\{ q\in S:p\cdot\hat{z}(q)\leq0\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Before we begin, notice that we have constructed a set of prices such that
 quantities chosen at the new prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, are still affordable under the old prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, note that for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is nonempty since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in F(p)$
\end_inset

 by strong or weak Walras' Law.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is lower semi-continuous, then the function defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(p,q)\equiv p\cdot\hat{z}(q)=\sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}\hat{z}_{l}(q)$
\end_inset

 is lower semi-continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the set 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is closed for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
 Also, since 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is bounded, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is compact for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now prove 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is FS-convex.
 Suppose for contradiction that there are some 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{m}\}\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 and some convex combination 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\lambda}=\sum_{t=1}^{m}\lambda_{t}p_{t}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\lambda}\notin\cup_{t=1}^{m}F(p_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\lambda}\notin F(p_{t})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,m$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}\cdot\hat{z}(p_{\lambda})>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,m$
\end_inset

, so 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}p_{t}\cdot\hat{z}(p_{\lambda})>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,m$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{m}\lambda_{t}p_{t}\hat{z}(p_{\lambda})=p_{\lambda}\cdot\hat{z}(p_{\lambda})>0$
\end_inset

, which contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 satisfies strong or weak Walras' Law.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 must be FS-convex.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Therefore, by the KKM Lemma, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\cap_{p\in S}F(p)\neq\emptyset
\]

\end_inset

Then, there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\cap_{p\in S}F(p)$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
 Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}=(1,0,\ldots,0)^{\prime},\ p_{2}=(0,1,0,\ldots,0)^{\prime},\ \ldots,\ \mbox{and}\ p_{L}=(0,\ldots,0,1)^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}\in S$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})=\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

, which means 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The two theorems above assume that the excess demand function is well defined
 for all prices in the closed unit simplex 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

, including zero prices.
 When preferences are stricly monotone, excess demand functions are not
 well defined on the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 so that the above existence theorems cannot be applied.
 Then, we need an existence theorem for strictly positive prices, which
 is given below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.4 (Existence Theorem I''').

\series default
 
\emph on
For a prive ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

, suppose the aggregate excess demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is defined for all strictly positive price vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, and satisfies the following conditions
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuous;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}:p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

 (interior Walras' Law);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is an 
\begin_inset Formula $s>0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p)>-s$
\end_inset

 for every commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{z}_{l}(p)\ \mbox{is bounded from below})$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}\rightarrow p$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}=0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max\{\hat{z}_{1}(p_{k}),\ldots,\hat{z}_{L}(p_{k})\}\rightarrow\infty
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

(The sequence of maximums diverges to infinity, not necessarily a particular
 sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})\}_{k=1}^{\infty}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Theorem*
Then there is 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $z(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

, and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
We are going to prove that when 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, then if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p)\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

, i.e., all goods are desirable.
 This theorem can be applied when preferences are strictly monotone.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Because of homogeneity of degree zero, we can restrict our search for an
 equilibrium in the unit simplex 
\begin_inset Formula $S\equiv S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Denote its interior by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

.
 We want to construct a correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 such that any fixed point 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\neq0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

, then by Walras' Law, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p)<0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l^{\prime}}(p)>0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l^{\prime}\neq l$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To start, define a correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F:S\rightarrow2^{S}$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(p)=\begin{cases}
\{q\in S:\forall\ q^{\prime}\in S,\ q\cdot\hat{z}(p)\geq q^{\prime}\cdot\hat{z}(p)\} & \mbox{if}\ p\in\mbox{int}(S)\\
\{q\in S:q\cdot p=0\} & \mbox{if \ensuremath{p}\ is on the boundary}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

Note, for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 means that, given the current 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

proposal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

counterproposal
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 assigned by the correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is any price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 that maximizes the values of the aggregate excess demand vector among the
 permissible price vectors in 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Here, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 can be thought of as a rule that adjusts current prices in a direction
 that eliminates the largest, but not all of the excess demand.
 The correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 assigns the highest prices to the commodities that are most in excess demand.
 In particular, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(p)=\left\{ q\in S:q_{l}=0\ \mbox{if}\ \hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})<\max\{\hat{z}_{1}(p_{k}),\ldots,\hat{z}_{L}(p_{k})\}\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

Observe that if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\neq0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

, then because of the interior Walras' law we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p)<0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l^{\prime}}(p)>0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l^{\prime}\neq l$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for such a 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, any 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)$
\end_inset

 has 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{l}=0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 (to maximize the values of excess demand vectors).
 Therefore, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)\subseteq\mbox{Boundary(}S)=S\backslash\mbox{Int}(S)$
\end_inset

.
 In contrast, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)=S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we want to show that the correpondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is an upper hemi-continuous correspondence with non-empty, convex, and
 compact values.
 First, 
\begin_inset Formula $S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 is compact by definition and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\cdot q$
\end_inset

 is a continuous linear function.
 Remember, a continuous function reaches its maximum on a compact set.
 Also, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is the set of maximals of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\cdot q$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, the set of maximals is a compact set, and since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\cdot q$
\end_inset

 is a linear function, its set of maximals is convex.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is compact and convex.
 By Weierstrass's Theorem, each continuous function on a compact set has
 a maximizer.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{Int}(S)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is non-empty since any price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 that maximizes the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is in 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is on the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}=0$
\end_inset

 for at least some good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, so there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot p=0$
\end_inset

, which also implies 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is also non-empty.
 Moreover, since 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a linear function and 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is defined to be the set of maximals of 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is compact and convex-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we show the correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is upper hemi-continuous, or equivalently, it has closed graphs, i.e., for
 any sequences 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}\rightarrow p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}\rightarrow q$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{t}\in F(p_{t})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)$
\end_inset

.
 There are two cases to consider.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 sufficiently large.
 From 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{k}\cdot\hat{z}(p_{k})\geq q^{\prime}\cdot\hat{z}(p_{k})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{\prime}\in S$
\end_inset

 and the continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

, we get 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot\hat{z}(p)\geq q^{\prime}\cdot\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{\prime}\in S$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Case
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is a boundary point of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Take any 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}>0$
\end_inset

.
 We want to show that for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 sufficiently large, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{lk}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, it must be that 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{l}=0$
\end_inset

; from this, 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)$
\end_inset

 follows.
 Take a sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{lk}\rightarrow p_{l}>0$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $k\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Then, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta>0$
\end_inset

, there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $N\in\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 such that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\geq N$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{lk}\in B_{\delta}(p_{l})$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{lk}>\epsilon$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 sufficiently large.
 Moreover, if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}$
\end_inset

 is on the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{lk}=0$
\end_inset

 by the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p_{k})$
\end_inset

.
 Instead, if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}\in\mbox{Int}(S)$
\end_inset

, then by conditions (iv) and (v), we must have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})<\max\left\{ \hat{z}_{1}(p_{k}),\ldots,\hat{z}_{L}(p_{k})\right\} \rightarrow\infty
\]

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 sufficiently large.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{lk}=0$
\end_inset

 by the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p_{k})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

.
 Before proving the inequality above, note that some prices go to zero as
 
\begin_inset Formula $k\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is a boundary point of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently, the sequence of maximums on the right-hand side of the expression
 approaches infinity as 
\begin_inset Formula $k\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 by condition (v).
 The left-hand side hand, on the other hand, is bounded from above.
 Now, since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{lk}>\epsilon$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 sufficiently large, then if the left-hand side is positive, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\epsilon\hat{z}_{l}(\hat{p}_{k})\leq p_{lk}\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})\iff\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})\leq\frac{1}{\epsilon}p_{lk}\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})
\]

\end_inset

By the interior Walras' Law, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}\cdot\hat{z}(p_{k})=0$
\end_inset

, and so 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{lk}\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})=0$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{lk}\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})=-\sum_{l^{\prime}\neq l}p_{l^{\prime}k}\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})$
\end_inset

.
 As a result,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{1}{\epsilon}p_{lk}\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})=-\frac{1}{\epsilon}{\displaystyle \sum_{l^{\prime}\neq l}}p_{l^{\prime}k}\hat{z}_{l^{\prime}}(p_{k})
\]

\end_inset

Finally, by condition (iv), there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $s>0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p)>-s\iff-\hat{z}_{l}(p)<s$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
 Hence,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
-\frac{1}{\epsilon}{\displaystyle \sum_{l^{\prime}\neq l}}p_{l^{\prime}k}\hat{z}_{l^{\prime}}(p_{k})<\frac{s}{\epsilon}\sum_{l^{\prime}\neq l}p_{l^{\prime}k}<\frac{s}{\epsilon}
\]

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{l^{\prime}\neq l}p_{l^{\prime}k}<1$
\end_inset

.
 Also, note that 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 is the bound in excess supply given by condition (iv).
 Thus, the sequence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p_{k})$
\end_inset

 is bounded from above, and it is less than the sequence of of maximums,
 which approaches infinity.
 In summary, for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}$
\end_inset

 close enough to the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

, the maximal demand corresponds to some of the commoditites whose price
 is close to zero.
 Therefore, we conclude that, for large 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, any 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{k}\in F(p_{k})$
\end_inset

 will put nonzero weight only on commodities whose prices approach zero
 and zero weight on all commodities whose prices don't approach zero.
 This guarantees that 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot p=0$
\end_inset

 and so 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 must be upper hemi-continuous.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Thus, the correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is an upper hemi-continuous correspondence with nonempty, convex, and compact
 values.
 Therefore, by Kakutani's Fixed Point Theorem, we conclude that there is
 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we show that any fixed point 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 cannot be a boundary point of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot p>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot q=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)$
\end_inset

 cannot occur simultaneously, so 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mbox{int}(S)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}(p^{*})<0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{k}(p^{*})>0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 by the interior Walras' Law.
 Thus, for such a 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

, any 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}\in F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 must have 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{l}^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum of the function 
\begin_inset Formula $q\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

, which means 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 is a subset of boundary points of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 But, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not in the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p^{*})$
\end_inset

, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Conditions for Walras' Law to be true
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the above theorems, Walras' Law is important to prove the existence
 of a competitive equilibrium.
 Under which conditions is Walras' Law held?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When each consumer's budget constraint holds with equality:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot x_{i}(p)=p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)
\]

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p\cdot x_{i}(p) & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p\cdot w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}p\cdot y_{j}(p)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}(p)-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}y_{j}(p)\right]=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, as long as the individual constraints hold with equality, Walras'
 Law must hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The above existence theorems on competitive equilibrium are based on the
 assumptions that the aggregate excess demand correspondence is single-valued
 and satisfies Walras' Law.
 The questions are under what conditions on economic environments a budget
 constraint holds with equality, and the aggregate excess demand correspondence
 is single-valued or convex-valued? The following various types of monotonicitie
s and convexities of preferences with the first one strongest and the last
 one weakest may be used to answer these questions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Types of monotonicity conditions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strict monotonicity: For any two consumption market bundles 
\begin_inset Formula $(x\geq x^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Monotonicity: 
\begin_inset Formula $x>x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Local non-satiation: For any point 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and any neighborhood, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(x)$
\end_inset

, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in N(x)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succ_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Non-satiation: For any 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succ_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 7.4.4.

\series default
 Monotonicity of preferences can be interpreted as individuals' desires
 for goods: the more, the better.
 Local non-satiation means individuals' desires are unlimited.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*

\series bold
Types of convexities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strict convexity: For any 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\equiv\lambda x+(1-\lambda)x^{\prime}\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Convexity: If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\equiv\lambda x+(1-\lambda)x^{\prime}\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Weak convexity: If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The above conditions are ordered by increasing weakness, i.e., each of the
 above condition implies the next one, the converse may not be true by examining
 thick indifference curves and linear indifference curves, strictly convex
 indifference curves as showed in figures.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 7.4.5.
 
\series default
The convexity of preferences implies that people want to diversify their
 consumptions and thus, convexity can be viewed as the formal expression
 of a basic measure of economic markets for diversification.
 Note that the strict convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to the strict quasi-concavity of utility functions 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 It also implies the convential diminishing marginal rates of substitution
 (MRS).
 Weak convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is equivalent to the quasi-concavity of utility funcitons 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Also, notice that the continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a sufficient condition for the continuous utility representations, i.e.,
 it guarantees the existence of a continuous utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(\cdot)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 7.4.6.

\series default
 Under the convexity of preferences 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, non-satiation implies local non-satiation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies convexity and non-satiation.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\in\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 By non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x^{\prime}\in X_{i}:x^{\prime}\succ_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\equiv\lambda x^{\prime}+(1-\lambda)x$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 By convexity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda\in(0,1):x_{\lambda}\succ_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \lambda\in(0,1):x_{\lambda}\in B_{\epsilon}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies local non-satiation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Strong or Interior Walras' Law holds depending on whether or not the aggregate
 excess deman is well defined under one of the two forms, and the additional
 given assumptions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.1.
 
\series default
\emph on
Under local non-satiation, we have the budget constraint holds with equality,
 and thus Walras' Law holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies local non-satiation.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 be agent 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's demand correspondence under utility maximization.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

 be firm 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

's supply correspondence under profit maximization.
 First, we claim that the budget constraint holds with equality.
 Suppose for contradiction that it does not, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ x\in x_{i}(p):x\in\mbox{Int}(B_{i}(p))$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Int}(B_{i}(p))$
\end_inset

 is an open set, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \epsilon>0:B_{\epsilon}(x)\subseteq\mbox{Int}(B_{i}(p))$
\end_inset

.
 By local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ x^{\prime}\in B_{\epsilon}(x):x^{\prime}\succ_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, the budget constraint holds with equality.
 Now, summing over 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot x_{i}(p)=\sum_{i=1}^{N}p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{N}\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)}\\
p\cdot\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}x_{i}(p)-\sum_{i=1}^{N}w_{i}-\sum_{j=1}^{J}y_{j}(p)\right]=0\\
p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Therefore, Walras' Law holds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.2.

\series default
 
\emph on
Under the strict convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 becomes a (single-valued) function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies strict convexity.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
 Suppose for contradiction that it is not.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ \{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq x_{i}(p):x\neq x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, which is equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
 Without loss of generality, consider 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\equiv\lambda x+(1-\lambda)x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\in B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 because
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p\cdot\lambda x+p\cdot(1-\lambda)x^{\prime} & \leq & p\cdot\lambda\left[w_{i}+\sum_{j\in J}\theta_{ij}y_{j}(p)\right]+p\cdot(1-\lambda)\left[w_{i}+\sum_{j\in J}\theta_{ij}y_{j}(p)\right]\\
 & = & p\cdot\lambda w_{i}+\lambda\sum_{j\in J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)+p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j\in J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)-p\cdot\lambda w_{i}-\lambda\sum_{j\in J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)\\
 & = & p\cdot w_{i}+\sum_{j=1}^{J}\theta_{ij}p\cdot y_{j}(p)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

 Finally, by strict convexity, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda\in(0,1):x_{\lambda}\succ_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\in x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is single-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.3.
 
\series default
\emph on
Under the weak convexity of preferences, the demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 satisfies weak convexity.
 We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x,x^{\prime}\}\subseteq x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 Then, by definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{\prime}\succsim_{i}x$
\end_inset

.
 Let us consider 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\equiv\lambda x+(1-\lambda)x^{\prime},\ \lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, because 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\lambda x+p\cdot(1-\lambda)x^{\prime}\leq p\cdot w_{i}+p\cdot\sum_{j\in J}\theta_{ij}y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\in B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

.
 By weak convexity, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\succsim_{i}x^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{\lambda}\in x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

, and thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is convex-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.4.
 
\series default
\emph on
If 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is compact (i.e., closed and bounded) and strictly covex, then the supply
 correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a well defined single-valued and continuous function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 is compact.
 By the maximum theorem, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a non-empty valued upper hemi-continuous correspondence by the compactness
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 (by noting that 
\begin_inset Formula $0\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

) for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, we show it is single-valued.
 Suppose that it is not.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j2}$
\end_inset

 are two profit maxizing production plans for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y_{j1}=p\cdot y_{j2}$
\end_inset

.
 By the strict convexity of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \lambda\in(0,1):\lambda y_{j1}+(1-\lambda)y_{j2}\in\mbox{Int}(Y_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ t>1:t\left[\lambda y_{j1}+(1-\lambda)y_{j2}\right]\in\mbox{Int}(Y_{j})$
\end_inset

.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $t\left[\lambda p\cdot y_{j1}+(1-\lambda)p\cdot y_{j2}\right]=tp\cdot y_{j1}>p\cdot y_{j1}$
\end_inset

, which contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j1}$
\end_inset

 is a profit maximizing production plan.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a single-valued function.
 Therefore, by the upper hemi-continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

, we know it is a single-valued and continuous funciton.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.5.

\emph on
 
\series default
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuous, strictly convex, locally non-satiated, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}>0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous single-valued function and satisfies the budget constraint
 with equality for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently, Walras' Law is satisfied for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
The proposition may not hold for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuous, strictly convex, and locally non-satiated.
 First, note that since 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}>0$
\end_inset

, then one can show that the budget constrained set 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous correspondence with non-empty and compact values and 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 is continuous.
 Then, by the maximum theorem, we know the demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 is upper hemi-continuous.
 Futhermore, by the strict convexity of preferences, it is single-valued
 and continuous.
 Finally, by local non-satiation, we know the budget constraint holds with
 equality, and thus, Walras' Law is satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, there is no utility function representation when preferences
 are lexicographic.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are continuous if the upper contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{w}(x_{i})\equiv\left\{ x_{i}^{\prime}\in X_{i}:x_{i}^{\prime}\succsim x_{i}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and the lower contour set 
\begin_inset Formula $L_{w}(x_{i})\equiv\left\{ x_{i}^{\prime}\in X_{i}:x_{i}\succsim x_{i}^{\prime}\right\} $
\end_inset

 are closed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the above propositions, we can have the following existence theorem
 that provides sufficient conditions directly based on the fundamentals
 of the economy by applying the Existence Theorem I''' above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.6.
 
\series default
\emph on
For a private ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

.
 there exists a competitive equilibrium if the following conditions hold
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are continuous, strictly convex, and strictly monotonic;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}>0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 are compact, strictly convex, 
\begin_inset Formula $0\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots J$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Proof
Important to note, we assume strict monotonicity, instead of a weaker condition,
 to guarantee the aggregate excess demand function is continuous.
 Then, by the assumptions imposed, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}(p)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{j}(p)$
\end_inset

 are continuous, single-valued, and homogenous of degree zero.
 Thus, the aggregate excess demand function is continuous, single-valued,
 and homogenous of degree zero.
 Also, by the strict monotonicity of preferences, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 satisfies interior Walras' Law, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}:p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, we only need to show that conditions (iv) and (v) of Theorem I'''
 are satisfied.
 The bound in (iv) follows from the non-negativity of demand (i.e., the fact
 that 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

) and bounded production sets.
 These conditions imply that a consumer's total net supply to the market
 of any good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 can be no greater than the sum of his initial endowment and upper bound
 of production sets.
 Finally, we show that condition (v) is satisfied.
 As some prices go to zero, a consumer whose wealth tends to a strictly
 positive limit (note that, because 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{w}>0$
\end_inset

, there must be at least one such consumer) and who has strict monotonicity
 of preferences will demand an increasingly large amount of some of the
 commodities whose prices go to zero.
 Therefore, by Theorem I''', there is a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Examples of Computing CE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
7.4.1.

\series default
 Consider an exchange economy with two consumers and two goods with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
u_{1}(x_{1})=x_{11}^{a}x_{12}^{1-a},\ 0<a<1;\ w_{1}=(1,0)\\
u_{2}(x_{2})=x_{21}^{a}x_{22}^{1-a},\ 0<b<1;\ w_{2}=(1,0)
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\nicefrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer 1's problem is to solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{1}}}\ u_{1}(x_{1})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{11}+px_{12}=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since utility functions are Cobb-Douglas types of functions, the solution
 is then given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
x_{11}(p) & = & a\\
x_{12}(p) & = & \frac{1-a}{p}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consumer 2's problem is to solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{2}}}\ u_{2}(x_{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{21}+px_{22}=p
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The solution is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{21}(p) & = & bp\\
x_{22}(p) & = & (1-b)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

By the market clearing condition,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{11}(p)+x_{21}(p)=1\iff a+bp=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus the competitive equilibrium is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p=\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}=\frac{1-a}{b}
\]

\end_inset

This is true because, by Walras' Law, for 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

, it is enough to show only one market clearing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 7.4.7.
 
\series default
Since the Cobb-Douglas utility function is widely used as an example of
 utility funcitons that have nice properties such as strict monotonicity
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

, continuity, and strict quasi-concavity, it is useful to remember the functiona
l form of the dmean function derived from the Cobb-Douglas utility functions.
 It may be remarked that we can easily derive the demand function for the
 general function: 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}(x_{i})=x_{i1}^{\alpha}x_{i2}^{\beta};\alpha>0,\beta>0
\]

\end_inset

by the suitable monotonic transformation.
 Indeed, since utility functions are invariant to monotonic transformations,
 we can rewrite the utility funciton as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left[x_{i1}^{\alpha}x_{i2}^{\beta}\right]^{\frac{1}{\alpha+\beta}}=x_{i1}^{\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}}x_{i2}^{\frac{\beta}{\alpha+\beta}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i1}(p)=\frac{\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}I}{p_{1}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{i2}(p)=\frac{\frac{\beta}{\alpha+\beta}I}{p_{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

when the budget line is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
p_{1}x_{i1}+p_{2}x_{i2}=I
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
7.4.2.
 
\series default
Consider an exchange economy with two consumers and two goods with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
u_{1}(x_{1})=x_{11}^{a}x_{12}^{1-a},\ 0<a<1;\ w_{1}=(1,0)\\
u_{2}(x_{2})=\min\{x_{21},bx_{22}\},\ b>0;\ w_{2}=(0,1)
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\nicefrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 Important to note, if 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{il}=0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, then a competitive equilibrium may not exist.
 However, in this example, a competitive equilibrium still exists under
 certain conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For consumer 1, we have already obtained
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{11}(p)=a
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{12}(p)=\frac{1-a}{p}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For consumer 2, his problem is to solve
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{x_{2}}}\ u_{2}(x_{2})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{21}+px_{22}=p
\end{equation}

\end_inset

At the optimal consumption, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{21}=bx_{22}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By substituting the solution into the budget equation, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
bx_{22}+px_{22}=p
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{22}(p)=\frac{p}{b+p}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{21}(p)=\frac{bp}{b+p}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Then, by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{11}(p)+x_{21}(p)=1$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
a+\frac{bp}{b+p}=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(1-a)(b+p)=bp
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(a+b-1)p=b(1-a)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{*}=\frac{b(1-a)}{a+b-1}
\]

\end_inset

To make 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 be a competitive equilibrium price, we need to assume 
\begin_inset Formula $a+b>1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
A necessary condition for 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 to be a CE is for 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 to be non-negative, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\geq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Existence of CE for Aggregate Excess Demand Correspondences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When preferences and/or production sets are not strictly convex, the demand
 correspondences and/or supply correspondences may not be single-valued,
 and consequently, the aggregate excess demand correspondence may not be
 single-valued.
 As a result, one cannot use the above existence results to argue the existence
 of competitive equilibrium.
 Nevertheless, by using the KKM lemma, we can still prove the existence
 of competitive equilibrim when the aggregate excess demand correspondence
 satisfies certain conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.7 (Existence Theorem II).

\series default
 
\emph on
For a private ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a non-empty, convex, and compact-valued upper hemi-continuous correspondence
 and satisfies Walras' Law, then there exists a competitive equilibrium,
 i.e., there is a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in S\equiv S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\cap\{-\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\}\neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $S\equiv S^{L-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Define a correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $F:S\rightarrow2^{S}$
\end_inset

 by,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(p)=\left\{ q\in S:p\cdot\hat{z}\leq0\ \mbox{for some}\ \hat{z}\in\hat{z}(q)\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

In this setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is the set of new prices 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 such that at least one consumption bundle chosen at 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is still affordable under the old prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)^{c}=\left\{ q\in S:p\cdot\hat{z}(q)\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Take 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)^{c}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(q)\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

 is open, then there is an open set 
\begin_inset Formula $O$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(q)\subseteq O\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 By the upper hemi-continuity of 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(q)$
\end_inset

, there is an open set 
\begin_inset Formula $N(q)$
\end_inset

 such that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in N(q)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(x)\subseteq O\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 So, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in N(q)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in F(p)^{c}$
\end_inset

, and hence, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(q)\subseteq F(p)^{c}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in F(p)^{c}$
\end_inset

, there is an open set 
\begin_inset Formula $N(q)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $N(q)\subseteq F(p)^{c}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)^{c}$
\end_inset

 is open, and equivalently, 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is closed.
 Also, since 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is bounded, then 
\begin_inset Formula $F(p)$
\end_inset

 is compact for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we prove 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 is FS-covex.
 Suppose for contradiction that there are some 
\begin_inset Formula $\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{m}\}\subseteq S$
\end_inset

 and some convex combination 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\lambda}=\sum_{t=1}^{m}\lambda_{t}p_{t}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\lambda}\notin\cup_{t=1}^{m}F(p_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\lambda}\notin F(p_{t})$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,m$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}\in\hat{z}(p_{\lambda})$
\end_inset

, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}\cdot\hat{z}>0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $t=1,\ldots,m$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{t=1}^{m}\lambda_{t}p_{t}\hat{z}=p_{\lambda}\cdot\hat{z}>0$
\end_inset

, which contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' Law.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 must be FS-convex.
 Therefore, by the KKM Lemma, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\cap_{p\in S}F(p)\neq\emptyset
\]

\end_inset

So, there exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in S$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\cap_{p\in S}F(p)$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in F(p)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in S$
\end_inset

, there is 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{p}\in\hat{z}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot\hat{z}_{p}\leq0
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we prove 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\cap\left\{ -\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\right\} \neq\emptyset$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for contradiction that this is not true.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

 is convex and compact and 
\begin_inset Formula $-\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is convex and closed, then by the Separating Hyperplane Theorem, there
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq0$
\end_inset

 and a value 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\cdot\left(-\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\right)<c<p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(-\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\right)$
\end_inset

 is a cone, which includes the zero vector, we must have 
\begin_inset Formula $c>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\left(-\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\right)\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $0<c<p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\cap\left\{ -\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\right\} =\emptyset$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})>0$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, which contradicts Walras' Law.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Similarly, we have the following existence theorem that provides sufficient
 conditions directly based on economic environments by apply the Existence
 Theorem II above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.8.
 
\series default
\emph on
For a private ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})$
\end_inset

, there exists a competitive equilibrium if the following conditions hold
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are continuous, weakly convex, and strictly monotone (strict monotonicity
 can't be weakened or a CE may not exist);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}>0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 are closed, covex, and 
\begin_inset Formula $0\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,2,\ldots,J$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Note*
The proof of this Theorem 7.4.8 is more technical than the one for Theorem
 7.4.6.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
7.4.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Existence of CE for General Production Economies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a general private ownership production economy
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
e=(\{X_{i},w_{i},\succsim_{i}\},\{Y_{j}\},\{\theta_{ij}\})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

recall that a competitive equilibrium consists of a feasible allocation
 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 and a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
(i)\ x_{i}^{*}\in D_{i}(p^{*})\equiv x_{i}(p^{*})\ \ \mbox{(utility maximization)}\\
(ii)\ y_{j}^{*}\in S_{j}(p^{*})\equiv y_{j}(p^{*})\ \ \mbox{(profit maximization)}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

We now state the following existence theorem for general production economies
 without proof since it is very complicated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.4.9 (Existence Theorem III, Debreu (1959)).
 
\series default
\emph on
A competitive equilibrium for the private-ownership economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 exists if the following conditions are satisfied
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 is closed, convex, and bounded from below;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are non-satiated;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are continuous;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 are convex;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\in\mbox{Int}(X_{i})$
\end_inset

 (Can't be weakened.
 If it is, the we won't have a general result, but a CE could still exist);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 are closed and convex (continuity and no IRS);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $0\in Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 (possibility of inaction);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $Y_{j}\cap\{-Y_{j}\}=\{0\}$
\end_inset

 (Irreversibility);
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\{-\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\}\subseteq Y_{j}$
\end_inset

 (free disposal).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*
7.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Uniqueness of Competitive Equilibria
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

So, we now know that a Walrasian equilibrium will exist under some regularity
 conditions.
 We worry next about the other extreme possibility: for a given economy,
 there are many equilibria so that we do not know what is the outcome of
 the market process.
 We can easily give examples in which there are multiple competitive equilibrium
 price vectors.
 When is there only one normalized price vector that clears all markets?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The free goods case is not of great interest here, so we will rule it out
 by means of the desirability assumption so that every equilibrium price
 of each good must by strictly positive.
 We want to also assume the continuous differentiability of the aggregate
 excess demand function.
 The reason is fairly clear; if indifference curves have kinks in them,
 we can find whole ranges of prices that are market equilibria.
 Not only are the equilibria not unique, they are not even locally unique.
 Thus, we answer this question while only considering the case of 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 differentiable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.5.1.
 
\series default
\emph on
Suppose all goods are desirable and gross substitutes for all prices (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial z_{h}(p)}{\partial p_{l}}>0,l\neq h$
\end_inset

).
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium price vector and Walras' Law holds, then it
 is the unique competitive equilibrium price vector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*

\family roman
\series medium
\shape up
\size normal
\emph off
\bar no
\strikeout off
\uuline off
\uwave off
\noun off
\color none
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial z_{h}(p)}{\partial p_{l}}>0,l\neq h$
\end_inset

 means that if we increase the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, we increase the demand for other goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Since all goods are desirable, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is another competitive equilibrium price vector that is not proportional
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:p\neq\alpha p^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $m=\max\frac{p_{l}}{p_{l}^{*}}=\frac{p_{k}}{p_{k}^{*}}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 (at least one 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 satisfies this, but there can be more).
 In this setup, good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 becomes the most relatively expensive good at prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
 Since all goods are desirable, then, by the definition of a competitive
 equilibrium, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})=\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

.
 By homogeneity of degree zero, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})=\hat{z}(mp^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Also, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $m=\frac{p_{k}}{p_{k}^{*}}\geq\frac{p_{l}}{p_{l}^{*}}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m>\frac{p_{h}}{p_{h}^{*}}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Note that if 
\begin_inset Formula $mp_{h}^{*}=p_{h}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq\alpha p^{*}$
\end_inset

 does not hold.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $mp_{l}^{*}\geq p_{l}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $mp_{h}^{*}>p_{h}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Now, if the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 stays the same when prices change from 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}^{*}=p_{k}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $m=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}^{*}\geq p_{l}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{h}^{*}>p_{h}$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 This means that when the price of good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is fixed, the prices of the other goods are down.
 As a result, we must have the demand for good 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 go down by gross substitutes.
 Hence, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{k}(p)<0$
\end_inset

, which implies 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{k}=0$
\end_inset

 by Walras' Law.
 However, we cannot have free goods, and this contradicts 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the aggregate demand function satisfies the Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference
 (WARP), and Walras' Law holds, then the competitive equilibrium is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
The Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference (WARP of the aggregate excess demand
 function): 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}:p\cdot\hat{z}(p)\geq p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{\prime})\implies p^{\prime}\cdot\hat{z}(p)>p^{\prime}\cdot\hat{z}(p^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
WARP implies that, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 could have been bought at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 was bought (so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)\succ\hat{z}(p^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is the optimal choice), then at price 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is outside the budget constraint (otherwise it contradicts the fact that
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 is the optimal choice).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

WARP is a weaker restriction than the continuous concave utility funciton.
 However, the restriction on the aggregate excess demand function is not
 as weak as it may seem.
 Even though two individuals may satisfy the individual WARP, the aggregate
 excess demand function may not satisfy the aggregate WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*

\series bold
\shape up
7.5.1.
 
\series default
\emph on
Under the assumptions of Strong Walras' Law and WARP, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq kp^{*}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
In this setup, quantities chosen at prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 are not affordable under the CE prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 By Walras' Law, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot\hat{z}(p)\geq p\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, by WARP, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)>p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq kp^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.5.2.
 
\series default
\emph on
Under the assumptions of Walras' Law and WARP of the aggregate excess demand
 function, the competitive equilibrium is unique.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
By Lemma 7.5.1, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ p\neq kp^{*}:p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)>0$
\end_inset

, which means at least for some 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}_{l}>0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
7.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Stability of Competitive Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The concept of competitive equilibrium is a stationary concept.
 However, it has given no guarantee that the economy will actually operate
 at the equilibrium point, or an economy may deviate from an equilibrium
 due to economic shocks.
 What forces exist that might tend to move prices to a market-clearing price?
 This is a topic about the stability on the price adjustment mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A paradoxical relationship between the idea of competition and price adjustment
 is that: if all agents take prices as given, how can prices move? To solve
 this paradox, one introduces a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Walrasian auctioneer
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 whose sole funciton is to seek for the market clearing prices.
 The Walrasian auctioneer is supposed to call the prices and change the
 price mechanically responding to the aggregate excess demand until the
 market clears.
 Such a process is called a Tatonnement adjustment process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\series default
A 
\series bold
Tatonnement Adjustment Process
\series default
 is defined, according to the laws of demand and supply, by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{dp_{l}}{dt}=G_{l}(\hat{z}_{l}(p)),\ l=1,\ldots,L
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{l}$
\end_inset

 is a sign-preserving function of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{l}(x)>0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x>0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{l}(x)=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x=0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{l}(x)<0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x<0$
\end_inset

.
 The above equation implies that when the aggregate excess demand is positive,
 we have a shortage and thus price should go up by the laws of demand and
 supply.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
\begin_inset Formula $p(t)$
\end_inset

: prices change over time; 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As a special case of, 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{l}$
\end_inset

 can be an identical mapping such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\dot{p}_{l} & = & \hat{z}_{l}(p)\\
\dot{p} & = & \hat{z}(p)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Under Walras' Law,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{d}{dt}(p^{\prime}p)=\frac{d}{dt}\left[\sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}^{2}\right]=2\sum_{l=1}^{L}p_{l}\cdot\frac{dp_{l}}{dt}=2p^{\prime}\cdot\dot{p}=p\cdot\hat{z}(p)=0
\]

\end_inset

which means that the sum of squares of the prices remain constrant as the
 price adjusts.
 So, the ratios of the prices change, but the length of the price vector
 does not.
 This is another price normalization.
 The path of the prices are resticted on the surface of a 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-dimensional sphere.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
7.6.1.

\series default
 An equilibrium price 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is globally stable if
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the unique competitive equilibrium,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{o}$
\end_inset

 there exits a unique price path 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\phi(t,p_{o})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq t<\infty$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\phi(t,p_{o})=p^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
We also define what it means for 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 to be locally stable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
7.6.2.

\series default
 An equilibrium price 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is locally stable if there is 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta>0$
\end_inset

 and a unique price path 
\begin_inset Formula $p=\phi(t,p_{o})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty}}\phi(t,p_{o})=p^{*}$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $|p^{*}-p_{o}|<\delta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The local stability of a competitive equilibrium can be easily obtained
 from the standard result on the local stability of a differentiate equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.6.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
A competitive equilibrium price 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is locally stable if the Jacobian matrix defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\left[\frac{\partial\hat{z}_{l}(p)}{\partial p_{k}}\right]
\]

\end_inset

has all real negative characteristic roots.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The global stability result can be obtained by Liaponov's Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Liaponov function.
 
\series default
A function 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 is said to be a Liaponov function for the differentiate equation system
 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{x}=f(x)$
\end_inset

 (differential equation) with 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x^{*})=0$
\end_inset

 (stationary point) if
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
There is a unique 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $V(x^{*})=0$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $V(x)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x\neq x^{*}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{dV(x)}{dt}<0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.6.2 (Liaponov's Theorem).

\series default
 
\emph on
If there exists a Liaponov function for 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{x}=f(x)$
\end_inset

, then the unique stationary point 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is globablly stable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Debreu (1974) has shown essentially that any continuous function which satisfies
 Walras' Law is an aggregate demand function for some economy.
 Thus, utility maximization places no restriction on aggregate behavior.
 Thus, to get global stability, one has to make some additional assumptions
 such as gross substitutability and WARP for aggregate demand functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma*

\series bold
\shape up
7.6.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Under the assumptions of Walraws' Law, desirability, and gross substitutes,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq kp^{*}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 is a competitive equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Quantities chosen at the new prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, are not affordable under the CE prices, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The general proof of this lemma can be seen in Arrow and Hahn, 1971, page
 224.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
7.6.3 (Arrow-Block-Hurwicz).

\series default
 
\emph on
Under the assumption of Walras' Law, if all goods are desirable and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 satisfies gross substitutability, or if 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 satisfies WARP, then the competitive equilibrium price is globally stable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Define a Liaponov function by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V(p)=\sum_{l=1}^{L}(p_{l}(t)-p_{l}^{*})^{2}=(p-p^{*})\cdot(p-p^{*})
\]

\end_inset

By desirability and gross substitutability or WARP of the aggregate excess
 demand function, the competitive price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is unique.
 So, there is a unique 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $V(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 In addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $V(p)>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq p^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 We now show that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is globally stable.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{dV}{dt} & = & 2\sum_{l=1}^{L}(p_{l}(t)-p_{l}^{*})\frac{dp_{l}(t)}{dt}\\
 & = & 2\sum_{l=1}^{L}(p_{l}(t)-p_{l}^{*})\hat{z}_{l}(p)\\
 & = & 2(p-p^{*})\cdot\hat{z}(p)\\
 & = & 2\left[p\cdot\hat{z}(p)-p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)\right]\\
 & = & -2p^{*}\cdot\hat{z}(p)<0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

by Walras' Law and Lemma 7.5.1 and 7.6.1 for 
\begin_inset Formula $p\neq kp^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, we have found a Liaponov function for the differentiate equation
 system 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot{p}=\hat{z}(p)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}(p^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
\shape up
 
\shape default
the unique stationary point 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 is globablly stable by Liaponov's Theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The above theorem establishes the global stability of a competitive equilibrium
 under Walras' Law and WARP, or under Walras' law, homogeneity, desirability,
 and gross substitutability.
 It is natural to ask how far we can relax these assumptions, in particular,
 gross substitutability.
 Since many goods are complementary goods, can the global stability of a
 competitive equilibrium also hold for complementary goods? Scarf (1961)
 has constructed examples that show that a competitive equilibrim may not
 be globally stable in the presence of complementary goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
7.6.1 (Scarf's Counterexample on Global Instability).

\series default
 Consider a pure exchange economy with three consumers and three commodities.
 Suppose consumers' endowments are given by 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=(1,0,0)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{2}=(0,1,0)$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{3}=(0,0,1)$
\end_inset

, and their utility functions given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{1}(x_{1}) & = & \min\{x_{11},x_{12}\}\\
u_{2}(x_{2}) & = & \min\{x_{22},x_{23}\}\\
u_{3}(x_{3}) & = & \min\{x_{31},x_{33}\}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Then, the aggregate excess demand function is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\hat{z}_{1}(p) & = & -\frac{p_{2}}{p_{1}+p_{2}}+\frac{p_{3}}{p_{1}+p_{3}}\\
\hat{z}_{2}(p) & = & -\frac{p_{3}}{p_{2}+p_{3}}+\frac{p_{1}}{p_{1}+p_{2}}\\
\hat{z}_{3}(p) & = & -\frac{p_{1}}{p_{1}+p_{3}}+\frac{p_{2}}{p_{2}+p_{3}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

 from which the only possible competitive equilibrium price system is given
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{*}=p_{2}^{*}=p_{3}^{*}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, the dynamic adjustment equation is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\dot{p}=\hat{z}(p)
\]

\end_inset

By Walras' Law, we know that 
\begin_inset Formula $||p(t)||=\mbox{constant}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 This means that the length of the prices doesn't change.
 Now, we want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \prod_{l=1}^{3}}p_{l}(t)=\mbox{constant}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Indeed,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{d}{dt}\left(\prod_{l=1}^{3}p(t)\right) & = & \dot{p}_{1}p_{2}p_{3}+p_{1}\dot{p}_{2}p_{3}+p_{1}p_{2}\dot{p}_{3}\\
 & = & \hat{z}_{1}p_{2}p_{3}+p_{1}\hat{z}_{2}p_{3}+p_{1}p_{2}\hat{z}_{3}=0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, we show that the dynamic process is not globally stable.
 First choose the initial prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}(0)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{3}}\left[p_{l}(0)\right]^{2}=3$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \prod_{l=1}^{3}}p_{l}(0)\neq1$
\end_inset

.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{3}}\left[p_{l}(t)\right]^{2}=3$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \prod_{l=1}^{3}}p_{l}(t)\neq1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{l=1}^{3}}\left[p_{l}(t)\right]^{2}=3$
\end_inset

 and the only possible equilibrium prices are 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{*}=p_{2}^{*}=p_{3}^{*}=1$
\end_inset

, the solution of the above system of differential equations cannot converge
 to the equilibrium price 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}=(1,1,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this example, we may note the following facts: (i) there is no substitution
 effect; (ii) the indifference curve is not strictly convex; (iii) the indiffere
nce curve has a kink and hence is not differentiable.
 Scarf (1961) also provided the examples of instability in which the substitutio
n effect is present for the case of Giffen's goods.
 Thus, Scarf's examples indicate that instability may occur in a wide variety
 of cases.
\end_layout

